  ["crypto.hash"] = {
    children = {},
    description = [[
Returns the digest of `data` under a hash function, as a vector.

- `algorithm` is `"sha256"`, `"sha384"`, `"sha512"`, `"sha1"`, or `"md5"`.
- `data` is the input. A string contributes its WTF-8 bytes. A number is converted to its textual form first.

The result is a vector of 32 bytes for SHA-256, 48 bytes for SHA-384, 64 bytes for SHA-512, 20 bytes for SHA-1, and 16 bytes for MD5. [`string.transcode`](/manual/api#string.transcode) writes it as hex or base64 text. SHA-1 is broken for collision resistance, so it is for formats that require it, such as git object names, and never for new signatures. MD5 is broken for every security purpose. It exists for formats that still name it, such as the `md5` password authentication of PostgreSQL and old checksum files.

An unknown algorithm raises `bad argument #1 to 'hash' (invalid option 'sha3')`. A `data` that is neither a string, a number, nor a vector raises `bad argument #2 to 'hash' (string or vector expected, got table)` with its type.

The following example hashes the test message of FIPS 180 with each algorithm and writes the digests as text.

```lus
local function hex(v)
    return string.transcode(v, "binary", "hex")
end
print(hex(crypto.hash("sha256", "abc")))
--> ba7816bf8f01cfea414140de5dae2223b00361a396177a9cb410ff61f20015ad
print(hex(crypto.hash("sha1", "abc")))       --> a9993e364706816aba3e25717850c26c9cd0d89d
print(#crypto.hash("sha512", "abc"))         --> 64
print(string.transcode(crypto.hash("sha256", ""), "binary", "base64"))
--> 47DEQpj8HBSa+/TImW+5JCeuQeRkm5NMpJWZG3hSuFU=
```

[`crypto.hasher`](/manual/api#crypto.hasher) computes the same digests over data that arrives in pieces. [`crypto.hmac`](/manual/api#crypto.hmac) computes a keyed digest with the same algorithms.]],
