  ["io.stdout"] = {
    children = {},
    description = [[
The standard output stream of the process as a file handle in text write mode.

The handle is open when the program starts, needs no permission, and is the default output that [`io.write`](/manual/api#io.write) writes to until [`io.output`](/manual/api#io.output) changes it. `print` writes to it as well. It accepts the methods of every file handle, such as `write`, `flush`, and `setvbuf`, and `io.stdout:write` returns the handle so that calls can chain. Closing it returns `nil` and `"cannot close standard file"`, and the handle stays open.

```lus
io.stdout:write("hello", ", ", "world", "\n")   --> hello, world
print(io.output() == io.stdout)                 --> true
```

[`io.stderr`](/manual/api#io.stderr) is the standard error, and [`io.stdin`](/manual/api#io.stdin) is the standard input.]],
    kind = "variable",
    module = "io",
    name = "io.stdout",
    origin = "lua",
    parent = "io",
    short_name = "stdout",
    since = "0.1.0",
    source = "stdlib/io/stdout.md",
    stability = "stable",
    type = "file",
  },
  ["io.tmpfile"] = {
    children = {},
    description = [[
Creates a temporary file, opens it for reading and writing in text mode, and returns its handle.

The function takes no arguments. It creates the file in the directory named by the `TMPDIR` environment variable, or in `/tmp` when the variable is unset, and removes its name from the directory at once. The file therefore has no path that another program could open, and its storage is released when the handle closes or the process ends.

On success the call returns one handle opened in mode `"w+"`. A file that cannot be created raises an error. The call requires `fs:write` for the path of the file it creates, so a grant limited to other paths raises an error such as `permission "fs:write" denied for '/tmp/lus_1f2e3d4c5b6a7980'`.

The following example writes to a temporary file, seeks back to its start, and reads the contents.

```lus
pledge("fs:write")
local tmp <close> = io.tmpfile()
tmp:write("scratch data")
tmp:seek("set")
print(tmp:read("a"))   --> scratch data
```

[`os.tmpname`](/manual/api#os.tmpname) creates a temporary file that has a name.

**Requires pledge:** `fs:write`]],
    kind = "function",
    module = "io",
    name = "io.tmpfile",
    origin = "lua",
    params = {},
    parent = "io",
    pledges = {
      "fs:write",
    },
    returns = {
      {
        type = "userdata|nil",
      },
    },
    short_name = "tmpfile",
    since = "0.1.0",
    source = "stdlib/io/tmpfile.md",
    stability = "stable",
  },
  ["io.type"] = {
    children = {},
    description = [[
Tells whether `obj` is a file handle and returns `"file"` for an open handle, `"closed file"` for a closed one, and `nil` for any other value.

`obj` is any value, and it must be present. A call with no argument raises `bad argument #1 to 'type' (value expected)`, while an explicit `nil` returns `nil`. The function requires no permission.

```lus
pledge("fs:write")
local tmp = io.tmpfile()
print(io.type(tmp))     --> file
tmp:close()
print(io.type(tmp))     --> closed file
print(io.type("file"))  --> nil
```

Handles from [`io.open`](/manual/api#io.open), [`io.popen`](/manual/api#io.popen), and [`io.tmpfile`](/manual/api#io.tmpfile), and the standard files, all count as file handles. The base function [`type`](/manual/api#type) returns `"userdata"` for each of them.]],
    kind = "function",
    module = "io",
    name = "io.type",
    origin = "lua",
