  ["network.tcp"] = {
    children = {
      "network.tcp.bind",
      "network.tcp.connect",
    },
    description = [[
The `network.tcp` table opens TCP connections with [`network.tcp.connect`](/manual/api#network.tcp.connect) and listens for them with [`network.tcp.bind`](/manual/api#network.tcp.bind).

A TCP socket has the methods `send(data)`, `receive(pattern, max_bytes)`, `settimeout(seconds)`, `getsockname()`, `getpeername()`, and `close()`. A server has the methods `accept()`, `settimeout(seconds)`, `getsockname()`, and `close()`, and `accept` returns a socket. A method other than `close` on a closed socket raises `"attempt to use a closed socket"`, and on a closed server `"attempt to use a closed server"`. A second `close` does nothing. The collector closes a socket or a server that nothing refers to any more, and one declared with the `<close>` attribute closes when its variable goes out of scope.

`connect`, `accept`, `send`, and `receive` wait for the network. Inside a coroutine given to `coroutine.detach` each of them suspends the coroutine until its socket is ready, and the other detached coroutines run in the meantime. Anywhere else they block their caller and run the detached coroutines while they wait. A timeout set with `settimeout` bounds each wait in both cases, and an operation whose timeout runs out raises `"receive timeout"`, `"send timeout"`, or `"accept timeout"` and leaves the socket usable.

```lus
pledge("network")
local server <close> = network.tcp.bind("127.0.0.1", 0)
server:settimeout(0.05)
local ok, err = catch server:accept()
print(ok, err:match("accept timeout"))   --> false  accept timeout
```]],
    kind = "module",
    module = "network",
    name = "network.tcp",
    origin = "lus",
    parent = "network",
    short_name = "tcp",
    since = "0.1.0",
    source = "stdlib/network/tcp/_module.md",
    stability = "stable",
  },
  ["network.tcp.bind"] = {
    children = {},
    description = [[
Binds a listening TCP server to `host` and `port` and returns the server.

`host` is a host name or a numeric address, and the server listens on the first address it resolves to. `port` is an integer from 0 through 65535, and port `0` lets the operating system choose a free port. `backlog` is the number of connections the system queues before `accept` takes them, and defaults to 128.

On success the call returns one server with the methods `accept`, `settimeout`, `getsockname`, and `close`. `accept()` waits for the next connection and returns a TCP socket. Every failure raises an error. A port out of range raises `bad argument #2 to 'bind' (port out of range)`, a name that does not resolve raises `"cannot resolve 'HOST': REASON"`, and an address that cannot be bound raises `"cannot bind to HOST:PORT: REASON"`. The call requires `network:tcp` for `host:port`.

The following example binds a port that the system chooses, accepts one connection, and reads a line from it.

```lus
pledge("network")
local server <close> = network.tcp.bind("127.0.0.1", 0)
local address, port = server:getsockname()
local client <close> = network.tcp.connect(address, port)
local conn <close> = server:accept()
client:send("ping\n")
print(vector.tostring(conn:receive()))   --> ping
```

[`tcp_server:getsockname`](/manual/api#tcp_server:getsockname) reports the chosen port, and [`network.serve`](/manual/api#network.serve) serves HTTP on a bound server.

**Requires pledge:** `network:tcp`]],
